## Another Month, Another Set of Surprises

### Salmonella Strikes Again

JCB Flavors, LLC has been on a roll--of the wrong kind--this June. With multiple recalls across its products due to potential Salmonella contamination, the company has become the latest poster child for why quality control matters. Items such as "White Cheddar Seasoning" and "Dill & Garlic Seasoning" have been flagged, and their distribution through popular retailers like Williams Sonoma raises concerns about the reach of such mishaps. Salmonella remains a frequent recall villain, with a whopping 172 cases reported this year.

### Allergen Mysteries

Labeling errors continue to haunt consumers. Market of Choice Inc's Vegan Kale Caesar Salad was found to contain undeclared sesame in its za'atar spice mixture. For those with sesame allergies, this oversight could spell trouble. With allergens being at the top of the recall reasons this year, one wonders when manufacturers will start taking label transparency seriously.

### Is Metal the New Crunch?

Straus Family Creamery's popular organic ice creams--cookie dough and mint chip--were both recalled due to potential metal contamination. Few things ruin the indulgence of creamy ice cream like the fear of biting into a shard of machinery.

### The Repeat Offender Problem

With 86 repeat offenders this year so far, one can't help but question if lessons are really being learned. Moonlight Packing Corporation, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, and Albertsons Companies LLC continue to lead the pack in the race to the bottom, each earning an 'F' for their recall record. Moonlight Packing tops the list with 11 recalls in total, all classified as Class I.

### By the Numbers

This year has seen a total of 543 recalls, divided into 173 Class I (serious), 334 Class II (less serious), and 36 Class III (minor). With 901 recalls recorded all-time, 2026 is shaping up to be a record-breaking year in all the wrong ways. A spike of 173 recalls in May alone showcases the industry's ongoing struggle with quality.

### Takeaway

It's not just about washing your produce or checking the expiry date anymore. With recalls spiking, consumers must stay vigilant, read labels (even when it seems redundant), and keep an eye on recall alerts to protect themselves and their families from potential health risks.

---

The recalls highlighted in this piece serve as a reminder of the importance of transparency and responsibility in food manufacturing. The safety of the food on our shelves hinges on manufacturers adhering to the highest standards.
